      "id": "comparison-tsla-ford-strategy",
      "category": "cross_company_comparison",
      "query": "Compare Tesla's autonomous service strategy with Ford Model e's technology focus.",
      "answer": "Tesla’s autonomous service strategy centers on operating its own ride-hailing platform, while Ford Model e’s focus is on EV and digital/connected vehicle technologies rather than a specific autonomous service.\n\n- **Tesla**: In June 2025, Tesla launched a **Robotaxi service**, described as “an autonomous ride-hailing platform that harnesses our technology and vehicles.” Tesla expects this service, together with its **Full Self-Driving (FSD) (Supervised)** subscriptions, to “advance a service-driven business model based on AI, software and fleet-based profits.” The Robotaxi business currently uses Model Y vehicles and is planned to expand to **Cybercab**, a “purpose-built autonomous vehicle.” [TSLA-2025-CHUNK-000003]\n\n- **Ford Model e**: The Ford Model e segment is defined by the **sale of EVs (including EREVs), service parts, accessories, and digital services**, and it “focuses on developing EV and digital vehicle technologies, as well as software development.” It also “provides software and connected vehicle technologies on behalf of the enterprise” and manufactures certain EVs, including for Ford Pro. Its operations span North America, Europe, and China. [F-2025-CHUNK-000005]\n\nBased on these disclosures, Tesla’s strategy explicitly targets an **autonomous ride-hailing services business and AI/software-driven fleet profits**, whereas Ford Model e’s technology focus is on **developing EV, digital, software, and connected-vehicle technologies and selling related products and services**, without a specifically described autonomous ride-hailing platform in the excerpt. [TSLA-2025-CHUNK-000003][F-2025-CHUNK-000005]",
